Logic models in Verilog-AMS

== builtin primitives

Gnucap provices logic primitives inherited from Verilog-95.

$ gnucap builtin.gc

== user defined primitives (UDP)

Logic primitives may be instanciated in compiled modules. In addition, user
defined primitives are supported.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc udp.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o udp.so
$ gnucap udp.gc

== continuous assignment

Similar to UDP instances, continuous assignments may drive nets. A continuous
assignment modelled in terms of an expression that depends on net values.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc latch.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o latch.so
$ gnucap latch.gc

== procedural assignment

(not implemented)

== cross and edge events

Device behaviour may be modelled in terms of crossing (analog) or edge
(digital) events. Events may trigger assignments that change device state.
(Note the "edge" (nonstandard) event syntax.)

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc event.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o event.so
$ gnucap event.gc

== event driven tasks

Events may also control the execution of tasks. There are builtin tasks (such
as $strobe), but tasks are really plugins and can be anything.

$ gnucap-mg-vams --cc strobe.vams | g++ -xc++ `gnucap-conf --cppflags` -fPIC -shared - -o strobe.so
$ gnucap strobe.gc
